<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Automat Chem</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Automatic Chemistry</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0142-0453</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">18925228</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2547778</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">S1463924689000040</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/S1463924689000040</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Evaluation of a new instrument offeringsodium, potassium, and ionized calcium incombination with haematocrit, pH and bloodgas analysis; the performance of the NovaStat Profile 1</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Schonfield</surname><given-names>Susan</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Frost</surname><given-names>Peter</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Cramb</surname><given-names>Robert</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Royle</surname><given-names>Chris</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group>        <aff id="aff"><label/>            <addr-line>Department of Chemical Pathology</addr-line>            <addr-line>Central Middlesex Hospital</addr-line>            <addr-line>Acton Lane</addr-line>            <addr-line>London</addr-line>            <addr-line>NWIO 7NS</addr-line>            <country>UK</country>        </aff>                     <pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>1989</year></pub-date><volume>11</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>22</fpage><lpage>27</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1989 Hindawi Publishing Corporation.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1989</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</copyright-holder><license license-type="open-access" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/"><p>This is an open access article distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</p></license></permissions><abstract><p>The Nova Stat Profile 1 analyser, a combined sodium, potassium,ionized calcium, haematocrit and conventional blood gas analyser,was evaluated over a four month period. In addition to assessingand demonstrating that the instrument met analytical requirements,an appreciation of the use of ionised calcium (iCa) was made.Prospective costs were characterised and practical problems of iCameasurement addressed.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>